<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-08">
    <title>Week 8</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ddo">
        <title>Chapter 11</title>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 11 covers 11.1, 11.2, and the complex numbers notes (or note 11.1 if you have seen complex numbers already)</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 12 Covers Note 11.3, Note 11.4, and Section 11.5.</alert>
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 11.1-11.2 are a review of simple harmonic motion and the
                        different ways to describe a sinusoidal function, each of which
                        has its own benefits. As we discussed previously, a mass on a spring
                        is a useful model for lots of different situations, and that's what
                        makes it worth spending a whole chapter on it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We'll take a detour to talk about complex numbers before coming
                        back to the differential equation of this chapter. Complex numbers
                        are extremely important in physics and simplify some algebra, so it's
                        worth learning about them.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 11.3 describes the motion of a harmonic oscillator with
                        a common type of damping. Again, having the solution allows us a good
                        deal of understanding of the motion. The solution path using complex
                        numbers will come up again in electrodynamics, quantum mechanics, <ellipsis/>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 11.4 introduces a driving force to the picture, and this is
                        where complex numbers shine. Solving (11.25) with the guess (11.26) is
                        a mess of trig functions, but the complex number method is much easier.
                        The important thing here is the shape of the resonance curve and the fact
                        that it is almost entirely controlled by the damping parameter. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 11.5 and 11.6 are fun, but optional. Section 11.5 is easier to
                        understand once you have learned to solve differential equations, and it
                        makes sense to wait until then.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>